\title{Remarks on the permutation representation}
\taxon{Remark}
\date{2021-10-01}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\ul{
\li{\p{\strong{(i)} \ref{GP29} gives us an equivalent definition of a group action. If #{G} is a group and #{X} is a set s.t. #{\Phi \colon G \to \mathrm{Sym}(X)} is a group homomorphism, then}
##{\rho \colon G \times X \to X, \quad (g, x) \mapsto \phi_g(x) \text{ where } \Phi(g) = \phi_g}
\p{is a group action. (Only one direction was proved here.)}}
\li{\p{\strong{(ii)} Using the notation of \ref{GP29}, by the First Isomorphism Theorem #{G/\ker\Phi \cong \operatorname{Im}\Phi \leq \mathrm{Sym}(X)}. Note}
##{\ker\Phi = \lbrace g \in G : \Phi(g) = \mathrm{id}_X\rbrace  = \lbrace g \in G : \phi_g(x) = \rho(g, x) = x\; \forall x \in X\rbrace  \trianglelefteq G,}
\p{i.e. all those elements that fix every element of #{X}, that act “trivially”.}}
}
